From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: omarchy-m5 <omarchy-m5@localhost>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 21:50:46 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 4/7] pmdomain: core: honour pd_ignore_unused in sync_state

Unchanged from omarchy-m5 patches/linux/0004 (applies as-is to
integration/aurora). With pd_ignore_unused on the command line,
genpd_power_off_unused() leaves unclaimed domains on, but the provider
sync_state paths still powered them off. On T6050 powering off an
unclaimed PMGR domain raises an SError (and would kill m1n1's framebuffer).
---
 drivers/pmdomain/core.c | 7 +++++--
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/pmdomain/core.c b/drivers/pmdomain/core.c
index a721a9363..c1398ecb3 100644
--- a/drivers/pmdomain/core.c
+++ b/drivers/pmdomain/core.c
@@ -3735,7 +3735,9 @@ void of_genpd_sync_state(struct device_node *np)
 		if (genpd->provider == of_fwnode_handle(np)) {
 			genpd_lock(genpd);
 			genpd->stay_on = false;
-			genpd_power_off(genpd, false, false, 0);
+			/* pd_ignore_unused: keep what the bootloader enabled */
+			if (!pd_ignore_unused)
+				genpd_power_off(genpd, false, false, 0);
 			genpd_unlock(genpd);
 		}
 	}
@@ -3763,7 +3765,8 @@ static void genpd_provider_sync_state(struct device *dev)
 	case GENPD_SYNC_STATE_SIMPLE:
 		genpd_lock(genpd);
 		genpd->stay_on = false;
-		genpd_power_off(genpd, false, false, 0);
+		if (!pd_ignore_unused)
+			genpd_power_off(genpd, false, false, 0);
 		genpd_unlock(genpd);
 		break;
 
